+
+### How to tell it worked
+
+- The commit hash the deploy platform reports as live matches the commit you
+  redeployed, not the one you rolled back from.
+  `<TODO: where that hash shows up — e.g. a field on the platform's dashboard>`.
+- The home page loads and shows the portrait, bio, and post list.
+- A post page loads. For a post with more than one translation, check that it
+  renders the translation matching the request's `Accept-Language` rather than
+  falling through to the post's default locale — see the fallback order in
+  `shared/resolve-translation.ts`. This is the part most likely to look "up"
+  but wrong if the rollback served a different build than intended.
+- No error-rate spike on `<TODO: monitoring/dashboard link>` in the minutes
+  after the redeploy.
+
+### What to check afterward
+
+- Whether the bad change is still the tip of `main`. Rolling back the deploy
+  does not roll back the branch — if nothing else changes, the next push to
+  `main` redeploys the bad build right back over your rollback. Either revert
+  the commit on `main` or hold the next deploy until it's fixed.
+- That CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) is green on whatever commit `main` ends
+  up pointing at.
